{"name":"Bermuda Triangle","hex":"#6f8c9f","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Bermuda Triangle","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["An area of the Atlantic Ocean where many ships and planes have disappeared under allegedly mysterious circumstances."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A confusing, chaotic place or situation where objects mysteriously disappear or things go wrong."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Bermuda_Triangle"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bermuda Triangle","blocks":[{"text":"The Bermuda Triangle, also known as the Devil's Triangle, is a loosely defined region in the North Atlantic Ocean, roughly bounded by Florida, Bermuda, and Puerto Rico. Since the mid-20th century, it has been the focus of an urban legend suggesting that many aircraft, ships, and people have disappeared there under mysterious circumstances. However, extensive investigations by reputable sources, including the U.S. federal government and scientific organizations, have found no evidence of unusual activity, attributing reported incidents to natural phenomena, human error, and misinterpretation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Bermuda_Triangle.png/330px-Bermuda_Triangle.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bermuda_Triangle","wikidata":"Q43708"},{"title":"Bermuda Triangle (Sea World)","blocks":[{"text":"The Bermuda Triangle was a themed indoor water ride at the Sea World theme park on the Gold Coast, Australia. In 2013, the ride was replaced by Storm Coaster, a Mack Rides water coaster."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Bermuda_Triangle_station_at_Sea_World.jpg/330px-Bermuda_Triangle_station_at_Sea_World.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bermuda_Triangle_(Sea_World)","wikidata":"Q4892661"},{"title":"Bermuda","blocks":[{"text":"Bermuda is a British Overseas Territory in the North Atlantic Ocean. The closest land outside the territory is in the US state of North Carolina, about 1,035 km (643 mi) to the west-northwest."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Flag_of_Bermuda.svg/330px-Flag_of_Bermuda.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bermuda","wikidata":"Q23635"},{"title":"Triangle","blocks":[{"text":"A triangle or trigon is a polygon consisting of three sides connected at three corners. It is one of the basic shapes in geometry and the simplest of polygons. The corners, also called vertices, are zero-dimensional points while the sides connecting them, also called edges, are one-dimensional line segments. A triangle has three internal angles, each one bounded by a pair of adjacent sides; the sum of angles of a triangle always equals a straight angle. The triangle is a plane figure and its interior is a planar region. Sometimes an arbitrary edge is chosen to be the base, in which case the opposite vertex is called the apex; the shortest segment between the base and apex is the height. The area of a triangle equals one-half the product of height and base length."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Triangle_illustration.svg/330px-Triangle_illustration.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Triangle","wikidata":"Q19821"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Gulfstream1.jpg/500px-Gulfstream1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Gulfstream1.jpg/500px-Gulfstream1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"False-color image of the Gulf Stream flowing north through the western Atlantic Ocean (NASA)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGulfstream1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Atlantic_hurricane_tracks.jpg/500px-Atlantic_hurricane_tracks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Atlantic_hurricane_tracks.jpg/1280px-Atlantic_hurricane_tracks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Atlantic_hurricane_tracks.jpg/500px-Atlantic_hurricane_tracks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tracks of all known Atlantic hurricanes between 1851 and 2019. Many storms pass through the Bermuda Triangle.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAtlantic_hurricane_tracks.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/The_missing_Training_Ship%2C_HMS_%27Atlanta%27_-_The_Graphic_1880.jpg/500px-The_missing_Training_Ship%2C_HMS_%27Atlanta%27_-_The_Graphic_1880.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/The_missing_Training_Ship%2C_HMS_%27Atlanta%27_-_The_Graphic_1880.jpg/500px-The_missing_Training_Ship%2C_HMS_%27Atlanta%27_-_The_Graphic_1880.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"HMS Atalanta. The Graphic, 1880","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_missing_Training_Ship%2C_HMS_'Atlanta'_-_The_Graphic_1880.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Deering2.jpg/330px-Deering2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Deering2.jpg/330px-Deering2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The schooner Carroll A. Deering, as seen from the Cape Lookou